# Use jobs from TypeScript

Jobs are private participant execution. Declare a `job` resource in native IDL,
then use generated `service.jobs.<name>` handles to register handlers and create
work. Callers that need to observe a workflow use an Operation instead.

```trellis
model ReconcileInput { orderId: string; }
model ReconcileOutput { changed: bool; }

service OrdersService {
  job reconcile {
    title "Reconcile order";
    description "Repairs one order projection.";
    payload ReconcileInput;
    result ReconcileOutput;
    retry { attempts 3; backoff [5s, 30s]; }
    key_concurrency { path orderId; policy queue; }
  }
}
```

Run `trellis generate`, then register the handler before waiting on the service:

```ts
import { Result } from "@qlever-llc/trellis";
import { RetryJobError } from "@qlever-llc/trellis/service";

service.jobs.reconcile.handle(async ({ job }) => {
  const result = await reconcile(job.payload);
  return result
    ? Result.ok(result)
    : Result.err(new RetryJobError({ message: "reconcile unavailable" }));
});

const job = await service.jobs.reconcile.create({ orderId }).orThrow();
```

Progress, structured logs, retry, and dead-job lifecycle are always available.
The default is five total deliveries with delays of 5s, 30s, 2m, and 10m.
Explicit retry supplies attempts and exactly attempts-minus-one positive delays.
Delivery progress acknowledgement is automatic; there is no authored heartbeat,
ack-wait, max-delivery, or feature switch.

For keyed work, the handler does not start until the declared key slot is
acquired. Waiting workers maintain delivery progress without spending a retry or
reaching max-delivery exhaustion. A `RetryJobError` returned by ordinary handler
execution does consume the configured attempts and backoff schedule.

Execution is at least once. Use stable job identity and idempotent side effects.
Typed updates are transient; durable progress, logs, result/error, and dead
lifecycle remain authoritative.
